Elastic Container Service (ECS) 🪣

👉The Elastic Container Service (ECS) on AWS is a great way to get up and running with containers. 
👉ECS offers a no added cost control plane with EC2 and Fargate serverless compute options. 
👉With ECS you can build highly scalable services and pay for what you use. 
👉You can store your container images using the Elastic Container Registry (ECR) and you can setup load balancers for traffic management.

👉With ECS you can use multiple strategies to determine when to scale up or down the number of container instances. 
👉This series of articles from Utkarsh Rastogi provides great insights into getting started with ECS.

👉One good option for running containers on AWS is with the Elastic Container Service (ECS). 
👉When debugging applications you likely will want to be able to access the container where it's running. 
👉With Fargate compute on ECS you can do this using the ECS exec setup.

👉The Elastic Container Service (ECS) is one of the main solutions on AWS to run your container workloads with. 
👉It supports using EC2 worker nodes that you have to manage or Fargate compute that is managed by AWS.

